        <div id="why-redwood-fades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Redwood Loses Its Color So Fast
          </h2>

          <p>
            Redwood heartwood gets its color and much of its natural decay resistance from extractives, a group of compounds that includes tannins. They are concentrated near the surface of a freshly milled board. Ultraviolet light breaks them down within weeks of exposure, and the board shifts from red toward a lighter tan. That first change is the fastest, which is why owners who wait a year to seal a new redwood deck are often disappointed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rain does the second part. Water carries the broken-down extractives out of the surface, sometimes leaving dark brown streaks where it runs off or pools. On boards near the ground or under drips from a roof edge, those streaks can be quite dark. Over the next year or two the surface fibers themselves weather, and the board settles into the silver gray that all untreated wood eventually reaches.
          </p>

          <p>
            A clear sealer slows the water side of this process. It keeps rain from soaking in and leaching extractives, and it reduces checking and cupping. It does very little about UV, so the color still fades. That is why clear redwood finishes often disappoint: they keep the wood dry and sound but rarely keep it red.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pigment is what changes the outcome. A finish with transparent red or brown iron oxide pigment blocks a share of UV at the surface and adds back some of the color the sun takes. The finish itself then fades over time rather than the wood beneath it, and a maintenance coat restores both protection and color. That is the logic behind every top pick on this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate sets the pace. In coastal fog belts, redwood fades slowly and mildew is the bigger threat. At altitude or in desert sun, a new deck can lose most of its red in a single summer. South and west facing boards, stair treads and the strip of deck in front of a sliding door fade first, so those are the areas to watch when judging whether a finish is still doing its job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CORE IDEA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">To keep redwood red, use a finish that is a little red.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Clear finishes keep redwood dry but not colorful. A transparent redwood-toned finish blocks more UV and replaces color as it fades, so the boards still read as redwood after a few seasons.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="heart-and-sap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Heartwood, Sapwood and Why Boards Look Mismatched
          </h2>

          <p>
            Redwood boards come from two parts of the tree. Heartwood, from the center, is the red, decay-resistant material most people picture. Sapwood, from the outer rings, is cream to pale yellow and has far less natural resistance to rot. Lumber grades that include sapwood are cheaper, and many decks mix the two, which produces striped or blotchy boards.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better heartwood-only grades are commonly sold under names such as clear all heart or construction heart, while grades with sapwood are often labeled construction common or with a B prefix. The exact names vary by mill and region. If you are buying redwood for decking, ask the yard which grade you are getting, because it affects both appearance and how the wood needs to be finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            A toned finish is the easiest way to even out heartwood and sapwood. A redwood or cedar tint darkens the pale sapwood more than the already-red heartwood, which narrows the contrast. A pure clear does the opposite, highlighting the difference. If you want the boards to look consistent, test the tone on a sapwood board before committing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sapwood is also less durable. On a deck with sapwood boards, pay extra attention to finish coverage and drainage, and treat cut ends with an end-cut preservative during construction. Sapwood boards near the ground or in constant shade are the first to show decay.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many redwood decks also use pressure-treated framing under redwood decking. The framing needs its own care, including hardware rated for treated lumber, and if you build with treated boards anywhere visible, our guide on whether <a href="/can-you-stain-pressure-treated-wood/">you can stain pressure-treated wood</a> covers when those boards are ready to finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tone-choice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Clear, Redwood-Toned or Darker: Picking the Look
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Clear.</strong> Choose a clear only if you like redwood's natural shift to a tan or gray color and want to keep the wood dry while it happens. Clear finishes are also useful on covered porches and furniture that see little direct sun. On open decks, plan on a yearly recoat at least, and accept that the red will fade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Redwood tone.</strong> This is the default choice for most redwood owners. A transparent redwood tint adds back the warm color, blocks more UV than a clear and evens out sapwood. Expect the boards to look a little more saturated than bare new redwood when freshly coated, then to soften toward a natural redwood color as the finish weathers.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cedar or natural tones.</strong> These lighter tints suit owners who want a more golden, less red look, or redwood that has already faded and that they do not want to push back toward red. They still add UV protection, just with less color change.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Darker browns and semi-transparent stains.</strong> Deeper tones hide weathering, mismatched boards and old stains, and they last longer in heavy sun. They also cover more of redwood's character. If a deck has been badly weathered and partly replaced, a darker semi-transparent stain may be the realistic way to make it look uniform; our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a> cover that territory.
          </p>

