        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cleaning and Brightening Redwood Before Sealing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Redwood rewards gentle prep. Start with an oxygen-based deck cleaner, which uses sodium percarbonate to lift dirt and mildew without the fiber damage chlorine bleach causes. Wet the wood, apply the cleaner, let it work for the time on the label and scrub with a stiff synthetic brush along the grain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse with a garden hose or a pressure washer set to low pressure with a wide fan tip. Redwood is soft, and a narrow, high-pressure stream raises fuzz and carves the earlywood, leaving a washboard surface that absorbs finish unevenly. If fuzz appears after washing, a light sanding once the wood is dry knocks it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow with an oxalic acid brightener. Cleaners are alkaline and darken redwood; the brightener lowers the pH and restores much of the red. It also removes the dark blue-black stains that form when iron from steel fasteners or tools reacts with redwood's tannins. Those iron stains do not come out with cleaner alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prevent iron stains from coming back by using stainless steel fasteners on redwood. Hot-dip galvanized fasteners are acceptable in many places, but stainless is the safer choice for visible fasteners on redwood. Avoid steel wool and plain steel brushes, which leave tiny particles that turn into black spots after the first rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the wood dry fully before sealing, typically 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. A water drop that soaks in within a minute or two shows the surface is open and ready. Our picks for the <a href="/best-surface-cleaner-for-pressure-washer/">best surface cleaners for pressure washers</a> include attachments that make low-pressure rinsing more even across a whole deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="restoring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Restoring Gray or Neglected Redwood
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gray redwood is usually salvageable. The gray is only a thin layer of weathered fibers, and the red heartwood beneath is still there. A deck cleaner followed by a brightener removes much of the gray, and a redwood-toned finish brings back the color. It will not look exactly like new lumber, but it can look close.
          </p>

          <p>
            If an old film-forming finish is peeling, it must come off first. Use a deck stain stripper rated for the finish type, then a brightener to neutralize it. On redwood, avoid aggressive sanding with coarse grits, which removes the very layer that holds color. If you sand, use about 80 grit and keep the sander moving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black streaks that remain after cleaning are often iron stains from fasteners. A second pass of oxalic acid targeted at those areas usually removes them. Mildew spots that return quickly point to shade and poor airflow; trim plants and clear gaps between boards so the deck can dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the structure while the deck is bare. Push a screwdriver into boards near the house, around posts and on the stairs. Soft spots mean decay, more common in sapwood boards. Replace those boards with heartwood if possible, and expect the new ones to look brighter until they weather a little.
          </p>

          <p>
            New and old boards side by side are a common problem after repairs. A toned finish evens out the difference better than a clear. Some owners let new boards weather for a few weeks before finishing so the color difference is smaller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set expectations before you start. Restored redwood usually comes back a little lighter and more even than when it was new, and the first coat of a toned finish often looks brighter than the coats that follow. After one or two maintenance cycles, the color settles into a stable shade that is easy to keep up with a light coat every year or two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Applying a Redwood Finish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pick a mild, dry day with no direct midday sun on the boards. Most penetrating finishes list a range of roughly 50 to 90 degrees and need a rain-free window afterward. Early morning after the dew dries or late afternoon in shade are ideal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coat two or three boards at a time along their full length, using a pump sprayer, roller or pad, and back-brush each section to work finish into the grain. Redwood absorbs evenly when prepped well, but end grain and sapwood will drink more. Give them an extra pass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not flood. Anything the wood cannot absorb in the label's wait time should be wiped up with a rag or worked into drier areas. Excess oil on redwood stays tacky and darkens unevenly. If the label calls for a second coat, many oil finishes want it applied wet on wet, before the first has dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish railings and vertical parts first, then the floor, working toward an exit. Keep pets and people off for the label's dry time, and allow longer before replacing furniture and planters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Handle oily rags safely. Rags soaked with drying oils can heat as they cure and ignite. Lay them flat outdoors to dry, or seal them in a metal container filled with water before disposal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a wet edge at all times and plan breaks at natural stopping points, such as the end of a board run or a stair landing. Stopping in the middle of a board leaves a darker overlap line that remains visible for the life of the coat, and redwood's even grain makes those lines especially noticeable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-project" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Redwood Decks, Siding, Fences and Furniture
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Decks.</strong> Horizontal redwood takes the most sun and foot traffic, so it needs the most pigment and the most frequent maintenance. A redwood-toned penetrating oil, recoated every year or two, is the standard approach. Avoid solid stains and paint unless the deck is badly weathered or mismatched, because film over soft redwood tends to peel.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Siding.</strong> Redwood siding lasts longest of any redwood use because it sheds water and gets angled sun. A toned penetrating oil can go several years between coats on shaded walls. Recoat sunny elevations more often. Our <a href="/best-exterior-wood-sealer/">exterior wood sealer guide</a> covers products that suit whole-house siding.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Fences.</strong> Redwood fences are mostly vertical, so a toned finish lasts well. Large fences are easiest to spray; choose a product that tolerates spraying without lap marks. Our <a href="/best-fence-stain/">fence stain guide</a> ranks products for long runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Furniture and planters.</strong> Redwood furniture is softer than teak and accepts standard penetrating finishes. Apply thin coats and wipe off excess. For planters, finish only the outside, line the inside or use a liner, and check the label before using any finish where soil and edible plants will contact the wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hot tubs and wet areas.</strong> Redwood surrounds near hot tubs and pools stay wet often. Clean them more frequently, recoat when water stops beading and consider a water-based penetrating product near water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Coverage and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most penetrating redwood finishes list roughly 150 to 300 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth wood, and less on rough or weathered boards. A first application after restoration takes more product than a maintenance coat. Measure decking, railings and stairs separately; railings with balusters add a lot of surface area.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Planning figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">150-300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, typical</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$40-$70</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per gallon at the time of writing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-3 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical recoat on redwood decks</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Dry time before sealing</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Prices for the finishes on this list ranged roughly from 40 to 70 dollars per gallon at the time of writing, with five-gallon pails cheaper per gallon. Add 30 to 60 dollars for cleaner and brightener. A typical 300 square foot redwood deck with railings uses two to three gallons per coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Redwood itself is expensive, especially outside the West Coast. A gallon or two of finish every couple of years is small compared with the cost of replacing weathered, checked boards. Spending on a quality toned finish and gentle prep protects that investment.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hire out, ask for the brand and tone in writing, and confirm the contractor uses low pressure and a brightener on redwood. Aggressive washing is the most common way contractors damage redwood decks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy all the finish at once and mix cans together in a clean bucket before starting, a step painters call boxing. Tinted finishes can vary slightly from batch to batch, and boxing keeps the color consistent across the whole deck instead of shifting where one can ended and the next began.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Upkeep: Keeping Redwood Red Year to Year
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sweep regularly and keep leaves and debris off the boards. Wet leaves leach tannins and leave dark marks, and debris in board gaps holds moisture. A quick rinse in spring removes pollen and dirt that would otherwise grind into the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the water drop test each spring and fall. When water soaks in on the sunniest boards, clean, brighten and recoat. With penetrating finishes you can recoat only the areas that need it, such as stairs and the path to the door, because the new coat blends with the old.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not stack coats on wood that still beads water. The new finish cannot penetrate and sits on top, turning sticky and dark. This is the most common complaint about redwood finishes and is almost always a timing issue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the same product and tone on recoats. Switching brands or bases can change color and cause adhesion problems. Write down the product, tone and date for future reference.
          </p>

          <p>
            If redwood is part of a larger deck project, our <a href="/best-deck-sealant/">deck sealant guide</a> covers clear finishes, and our <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">deck stain rankings</a> cover longer-lasting pigmented options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting a year to seal new redwood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Redwood loses much of its red color in the first months of sun. Waiting a full season means sealing wood that has already faded. Unless the boards are wet or have mill glaze, clean and finish new redwood once it is dry and absorbs water, often within a few weeks of installation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using steel wool or steel fasteners</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Iron reacts with redwood's tannins and leaves blue-black stains, especially after rain. Steel wool fibers and plain steel nails are common culprits. Use stainless steel fasteners, synthetic scrub pads and brushes, and remove existing iron stains with an oxalic acid brightener before sealing. A single forgotten steel brush can spot an entire deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing redwood at high pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Redwood is soft, and a narrow, high-pressure stream raises fuzz and gouges the earlywood. The damaged surface absorbs finish unevenly and looks blotchy. Use a cleaner to do the work, keep the pressure low, use a wide fan tip and hold the wand well back from the boards. Fuzz can be sanded off once dry, but gouges stay.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a clear finish to keep the red</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear finishes keep redwood dry but do little against UV, so the red still fades toward tan and gray. If color is the goal, choose a redwood-toned or semi-transparent finish with transparent pigment, and refresh it when water stops beading. The lightest redwood tone is usually enough to make a visible difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the brightener after cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Deck cleaners are alkaline and leave redwood darker and duller. Skipping the oxalic acid brightener means sealing in that dull color and any iron stains. A brightener restores much of the natural red and neutralizes the cleaner, so the finish looks right and bonds well. Rinse it off thoroughly before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

