        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sealing redwood actually necessary?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Redwood heartwood resists decay naturally, so it can survive unsealed, but it will turn gray and check over time. Sealing slows water absorption, cracking and mildew, and a toned finish keeps the red color. Sapwood boards and decks in wet climates benefit even more. If you want redwood to look like redwood, a finish is necessary.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What color sealer should I use on redwood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most redwood, choose a transparent redwood tone. It adds back color, blocks more UV than a clear and evens out sapwood and heartwood. Lighter cedar or natural tones suit owners who want a golden look, and darker semi-transparent stains help on weathered or mismatched boards. Test on a sample board and judge it in sunlight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can I seal a new redwood deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Redwood is usually kiln-dried or air-dried before sale, so it can often be finished soon after installation once it is clean and dry. Do a water drop test: if water soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready. If it beads, there may be mill glaze or moisture; wait a few weeks or lightly sand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I get the red color back on gray redwood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean the deck with an oxygen-based cleaner, rinse at low pressure and then use an oxalic acid brightener. The brightener restores much of the red and removes iron stains. Once the wood is dry, apply a redwood-toned penetrating finish. The color will not match brand-new lumber exactly but can come close.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Thompson's WaterSeal on redwood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but it is not ideal if you want to keep the red. Clear water repellents keep redwood dry but offer little UV protection, so the color still fades. If you only want to slow water damage and do not mind graying, a clear repellent is fine. For color retention, choose a toned finish.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Oil or water base: which suits redwood better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many professionals prefer oil-based penetrating finishes on redwood because they soak in well, enrich the color and fade gradually. Water-based finishes dry faster, have less odor, clean up with soap and water and are legal in low-VOC states. Both can work if they carry enough pigment and are applied to clean, dry wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a redwood deck be resealed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most redwood decks need a fresh coat every one to three years, depending on sun, climate, foot traffic and the finish. Clear finishes often need yearly attention, while toned or semi-transparent finishes last longer. Siding and fences can go longer. Use the water drop test and recoat when water soaks in quickly.
              </p>
            </div>

